From 7092a6bc3f32c9f229f72b2911fc95d723b7f0ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 20:18:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 344/345] proc_macro: one child per proc-macro executable
 serves every invocation of the expand pass

py314-pydantic_core 2.46.5 src/serializers/type_serializers/bytes.rs:26:17: `BytesSerializer { .. }.into()` into
`Arc<CombinedSerializer>` = "Failed to find an impl of From<BytesSerializer> for CombinedSerializer" (next would be the
missing `impl TypeSerializer for CombinedSerializer`). enum_dispatch 0.3.13 links `#[enum_dispatch] pub enum
CombinedSerializer` (src/serializers/shared.rs:52, from `combined_serializer!` at :88) and
`#[enum_dispatch(CombinedSerializer)] trait TypeSerializer` (:398) through process globals (src/cache.rs: TRAIT_DEFS,
ENUM_DEFS, DEFERRED_LINKS) and emits the `From` impls and the trait impl from whichever invocation finds both halves.
rustc loads a proc-macro crate once per crate compilation, so the cache survives; mrustc spawned a child per
invocation, so neither half saw the other and nothing was emitted. Not a regression: it has always been so.

lib/libproc_macro `main`: with `MRUSTC_PROC_MACRO_SERVER=1` in the environment (and no input file) the process keeps
going after an invocation's output: it writes a "more" byte, reads the next request from stdin (a v128 length and the
macro name; EOF = exit), resets the span table (the compiler numbers definitions from 0 in every invocation) and
serves it. Macros' own globals persist, as under rustc. Without the variable (an older compiler) it serves one
invocation as before.

expand/proc_macro.cpp: a pool of idle children keyed by executable path, one child each. Once the output's end marker
has been read, one more byte decides: the "more" byte = the child goes to the pool, and the next invocation of its
executable sends the macro name as the request instead of spawning; EOF = a one-shot child (a plugin built against the
previous libproc_macro, every fakepm.py kit, `MRUSTC_PROC_MACRO_SERVER=0` in the compiler's environment) is reaped as
before. So a new compiler works with old plugins and vice versa. The compiler's pipe ends are close-on-exec: a later
child would otherwise hold the write end of a pooled child's stdin and that child would never see EOF. The pool is
closed at the end of `Expand()` (the children exit on EOF, then are waited for), before the memory-heavy passes; the
destructor closes its pipes before waiting, so a child still writing after an error gets EPIPE instead of blocking.
Windows stays one-shot. The `MRUSTC_DUMP_PROCMACRO` dumps hold one invocation's input as before (the request bypasses
them). Big crates also save a process spawn per invocation.

Assisted-By: Claude Fable 5.1
